// T4 gate: does an approved local actually get reclaimed, and safely?
//
// `p` is the shape the release decision approves, and nothing in the compiler
// freed it before T4:
//   condition 1  it does not outlive work
//   condition 2  bound to new(int), so work owns it
//   condition 3  no arena block
//   condition 4  function scope, bound once, never re-assigned
//
// Called in a loop, so the difference is measurable rather than theoretical.
// Without a release this leaks one 24-byte object per iteration (a 16-byte
// GOO_OBJ_HEADER plus an 8-byte int), which is the daemon's per-request shape in
// miniature. Measured before T4: 10,001 allocs, 1 free, 240,000 bytes in 10,000
// blocks definitely lost.
//
// IMPORT-FREE ON PURPOSE. `.handoff.md` records a local_escape table that read
// as confident and was conservative because the imports had not resolved. A gate
// whose verdict can move for an unrelated reason is not a gate.
//
// WHY NOTHING DERIVED FROM `p` LEAVES work. escape_expr_taint's AST_UNARY_EXPR
// arm returns the operand's taint for EVERY unary operator. That is right for
// `&x`, where the address aliases x, and over-conservative for `*p`, where the
// deref copies the pointee out. So `return *p` marks `p` escaping and the release
// is refused. Sound, never a bug, and it is why this probe increments a global
// instead of returning a value read through `p`. Recorded in the ledger; fixing it
// means changing the arm all three escape passes share.
package main

var count int

func work(n int) {
	p := new(int)
	*p = n
	count = count + 1
}

func main() {
	for i := 0; i < 10000; i++ {
		work(i)
	}
}
